# =============================================================================
# ENVIRONMENT
# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is git-ignored and must stay that way —
# it carries the session signing key.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOCAL (SQLite):
#   DATABASE_URL="file:./dev.db?connection_limit=1&pool_timeout=30"
#
# PRODUCTION (Neon or Supabase Postgres). Both give you two connection strings
# and BOTH are needed, for different reasons. See docs/deploying.md:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  — the POOLED connection (port 6543, via pgbouncer). Used by
#                   the running app. Serverless functions open a connection per
#                   invocation, and a direct connection per invocation exhausts
#                   Postgres' connection limit within minutes of real traffic.
#                   `pgbouncer=true` is required or Prisma's prepared statements
#                   break against the pooler.
#
#   DIRECT_URL    — the DIRECT connection (port 5432). Used ONLY by
#                   `prisma migrate`. Migrations issue DDL and advisory locks,
#                   which pgbouncer in transaction mode cannot carry.
#
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1"
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"
DATABASE_URL="file:./dev.db?connection_limit=1&pool_timeout=30"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sessions
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# At least 32 characters. The app refuses to sign a session with a weaker key
# rather than doing it quietly.
#
# Generate one:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Use a DIFFERENT value in production from the one on your laptop. Rotating it
# invalidates every live session, which is the point: it is what you do if a
# key is ever exposed.
AUTH_SECRET="change-me-to-at-least-32-random-characters"

# How long a signed-in session lasts. 8 hours by default — a working day.
SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=28800

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tenancy
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How a request finds its school.
#   path       /s/<slug>/login        — the default, works anywhere
#   subdomain  <slug>.example.edu     — production, when DNS allows
TENANCY_MODE=path

# Only read in subdomain mode. The deployment's own domain, with no protocol.
# ROOT_DOMAIN=example.edu

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# File storage
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Avatars, course materials and student submissions.
#
#   local  — writes under STORAGE_ROOT. Development only, and the app now
#            REFUSES to start with it when NODE_ENV=production: a serverless or
#            container filesystem is read-only or discarded on redeploy, so
#            "local" there does not fail, it accepts every upload and silently
#            loses it.
#   s3     — any S3-compatible store. Written against Cloudflare R2; works
#            unchanged against AWS S3, Backblaze B2 or MinIO.
STORAGE_DRIVER=local
# STORAGE_ROOT=./.storage

# S3 / R2. All four are required when STORAGE_DRIVER=s3, and the app names the
# missing ones together at startup rather than failing on the first upload.
#
# From Cloudflare: R2 -> Manage R2 API Tokens -> Create API Token, permission
# "Object Read & Write", scoped to the one bucket.
#
# LEAVE THE BUCKET PRIVATE. Nothing is served straight from it — every read goes
# through a route handler that checks the session and the reader's relationship
# to the record. A public bucket would be a second authority on who may read a
# file, answering over the open internet, that no route handler is consulted
# about and no audit log records.
#
# STORAGE_DRIVER=s3
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# S3_BUCKET=matricula-files
# S3_REGION=auto
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VIRTUAL CLASSES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Leave every one of these unset and the product still works: a teacher pastes
# a link from Teams, Zoom, Meet or anything else, and the class appears in the
# module with a time on it. That is the MANUAL provider, it is always available,
# and it needs no configuration.
#
# Set them to have the app create Microsoft Teams meetings itself.
#
# Register a MULTI-TENANT application in Entra with the application permission
# OnlineMeetings.ReadWrite.All, granted admin consent by each school. Then, in
# that school's tenant, an administrator must authorise the app to act for its
# organisers — Graph refuses app-only meeting creation without it:
#
#   New-CsApplicationAccessPolicy -Identity Lms -AppIds "<AZURE_CLIENT_ID>"
#   Grant-CsApplicationAccessPolicy -PolicyName Lms -Global
#
# Until all three are present, `automaticProvidersAvailable()` returns nothing
# and the UI offers only a pasted link rather than promising Teams and failing.
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Who organises a meeting when the teacher has no email address on file.
# AZURE_DEFAULT_ORGANISER=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMAIL
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Leave MAIL_DRIVER unset and messages are written to .mail/ instead of sent.
# That is fine in development and NOT fine in production: password reset is the
# one flow somebody uses because they are already locked out, and it does
# nothing useful until this is configured. The UI says so rather than claiming
# to have emailed anybody.
#
# Two providers ship with the app, both free at the volume a school sends and
# neither needing a dependency:
#
#   resend  — 3,000 messages a month free.  https://resend.com
#   brevo   — 300 messages a DAY free.      https://brevo.com
#
# MAIL_DRIVER=resend
# MAIL_API_KEY=
#
# The sender. THE DOMAIN MUST BE VERIFIED WITH THE PROVIDER — an unverified
# sender is the commonest reason transactional mail silently fails, and it fails
# by being rejected or filed as spam rather than by erroring. Both forms work:
#
#   MAIL_FROM=portal@yourschool.edu
#   MAIL_FROM=Your School <portal@yourschool.edu>
#
# MAIL_FROM=
#
# Why not SMTP through the mailbox that came with the domain: a mailbox is built
# for correspondence, not automation. No delivery reporting, no bounce handling,
# tight rate limits, and a reputation that collapses the first time a school
# sends four hundred password resets in a morning. SMTP can be added behind the
# same interface if a school insists on their own relay.
